<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">4 March 1933 · Washington, D.C.</p><h1 class="sp-title">Roosevelt: The Only Thing We Have to Fear</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Franklin D. Roosevelt took office at the lowest point of the Great Depression: a quarter of the workforce was unemployed, and banks were failing across the country as panicked depositors withdrew their savings. In his inaugural address he promised action and asked for broad executive power to meet the emergency.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>So, first of all, let me assert my firm belief that the only thing we have to fear is fear itself — nameless, unreasoning, unjustified terror which paralyzes needed efforts to convert retreat into advance.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">First Inaugural Address, 4 March 1933.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Franklin_D._Roosevelt%27s_First_Inaugural_Address" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Two days later Roosevelt closed every bank in the country for a 'bank holiday', and in his first hundred days Congress passed a flood of measures for relief and recovery. The New Deal reshaped the role of the federal government in American life, though full recovery came only with rearmament for the Second World War.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a></div></div></section>
